prob": 0.9972793459892273, "span": {"begin": 722, "end": 731}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:5727"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "PTCH", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.5883192420005798, "span": {"begin": 731, "end": 735}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6608"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SMO", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9902991056442261, "span": {"begin": 775, "end": 778}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3280", "9910"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "HH ligand", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.7660001516342163, "span": {"begin": 785, "end": 794}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:5727"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "PTCH", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9899358153343201, "span": {"begin": 804, "end": 808}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6608"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SMO", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.992371678352356, "span": {"begin": 825, "end": 828}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6608"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SMO", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9741021394729614, "span": {"begin": 845, "end": 848}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6608"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SMO", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9976881742477417, "span": {"begin": 996, "end": 999}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6469"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SHH", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9888255000114441, "span": {"begin": 1066, "end": 1069}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:2735"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "GLI transcription factors", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9777610898017883, "span": {"begin": 1288, "end": 1313}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:94"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "HH target gene", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.947513997554779, "span": {"begin": 1346, "end": 1360}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6608"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SMO", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9945322275161743, "span": {"begin": 1447, "end": 1450}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6469"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SHH", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9953222870826721, "span": {"begin": 1496, "end": 1499}}, {"id": ["mesh:D000013"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "birth defects", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9949020147323608, "span": {"begin": 1565, "end": 1578}}, {"id": ["mesh:C563481", "mim:166780"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "OFCs", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999611377716064, "span": {"begin": 1589, "end": 1593}}], "text": "The Smoothened (SMO) receptor is Class F G protein coupled receptor involved in signal transduction of the Sonic Hedgehog (SHH) pathway. It includes distinct functional groups including ligand binding pockets, cysteine rich domain (CRD), transmembrane helix (TM), extracellular loop (ECL), intracellular loop (ICL), and a carboxyl-terminal tail (C-term tail) (Arensdorf, Marada et al. 2016).&nbsp; SMO signaling is dependent upon its relocation to a subcellular location. This relocation occurs in the primary cilium (PC) in vertebrates (Huangfu and Anderson 2005). Relocation of SMO to the PC typically occurs within ~20 minutes of agonist stimulation (Arensdorf, Marada et al. 2016).   In the absence of SHH ligand, the Patched (PTCH) receptor suppresses the activation of SMO. When HH ligand binds to PTCH, suppression on SMO is released and SMO can relocate, accumulate, and signal to intracellular effectors (Denef, Neub&uuml;ser et al. 2000, Rohatgi and Scott 2007). It has been shown that SMO localization to the tip of the primary cilia is essential for the SHH signaling cascade in vertebrates (Corbit, Aanstad et al. 2005, Rohatgi, Milenkovic et al. 2007, Rohatgi, Milenkovic et al. 2009). This relocation then leads to signaling to effectors resulting in the activation of the GLI transcription factors and the subsequent induction of HH target gene expression (Alexandre, Jacinto et al. 1996, Von Ohlen and Hooper 1997). Antagonism of SMO disrupts the downstream signaling cascade of SHH and if disrupted during critical periods of development can lead birth defects including OFCs. &nbsp;", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:56:56 +0000 2026"}